# TASK-260715-ypo7yo — handoff evidence, round 6 (verdict-05 rework)

Revision **2026-07-28.r6**. Status: **ready for review**. No portal mutation has
occurred and none is authorized by this handoff.

Verdict 05 raised two blocking findings and characterised both as recoverable
autonomous metadata/contract rework needing no human decision. That is how they
were handled. **Both are CONFIRMED and closed.** Neither is an entitlement
decision: **no target row moved, no grant changed, no capability was added or
withdrawn.** Both are cases of a *statement about* the contract contradicting the
contract.

---

## 1. F1 — the authoritative JSON contradicted itself about the macOS Keychain grant

**CONFIRMED**, and reproduced before being accepted: the reviewer's own `jq` gate
run against the r5 artifact returns `false`, exit **1**. `crossPlatformRules[2]`
still read *"granted only where it is functional (rule K1): the iOS host and
appex, and the macOS host"* — the r4 contract — while the target rows, rule K2,
`keychainAccessGroups[0].consumedByTargets`, assertions A6/A17 and the r5 revision
log all said iOS-only. The r5 validator passed 846 checks because **no rule looked
at the summary at all**.

### The finding was widened before it was fixed

The reviewer's rework item 4 asked for the adjacent App Group wording to be
reviewed so the class would be closed rather than one string patched. So every
string in the r5 JSON was swept for a stale r4 grant or count claim. **Two**
survivors turned up:

| location | stale claim |
| --- | --- |
| `crossPlatformRules[2]` | the shared Keychain group granted to the macOS host — the reviewer's finding |
| `appGroupStyleRule.statement` | "one App Group record **per family**" — true of r4's two records, false since r5 deleted the probe record |

Two independent instances in one artifact is a class, not a typo. Hence the fix
below is a rule over all six sentences plus a count scan.

### The fix — rule S1, gate R32, gate R33

`crossPlatformRules` stays a plain array of strings, so the reviewer's gate keeps
working verbatim. What is new is that **every sentence is now a checked projection
of the data**:

- new `crossPlatformSharingContract` (**rule S1**) carries one structured rule per
  rendered sentence — `entitlementKey`, `mentionStyle`, `grantedTargets`,
  `grantClause`, `nonGranteeMentions`, `records`, `derivation`;
- **R32** derives the grant set from the target rows, cross-checks it against the
  record's own `consumedByTargets`, then requires the `grantClause` **verbatim** in
  the sentence, requires the target names *inside* the clause to equal the derived
  set, and requires **every other target named anywhere in the sentence** to be a
  declared non-grantee mention carrying its own clause. Display names are derived
  from the rows (`macos.host` → "macOS host"), and R32 also proves no display name
  is a substring of another, so a mention cannot be mis-attributed;
- **R33** requires the same grant clauses to appear in the rationale document,
  whitespace-normalised — hard wrapping is allowed, rewording is not — plus the
  current revision string. The JSON can no longer be corrected while the prose is
  left asserting the superseded grant, one file over;
- the count half is closed by a scan: any *"N App Group record(s)"* claim anywhere
  in the JSON must sit at a **registered** path and equal the declared record
  count. A registered path that stops stating a count fails too, so the register
  cannot rot, and a historical exclusion that resolves to nothing fails, so the
  allowlist cannot quietly accumulate dead entries.

### The new rule found a third instance on its first run

R32's count scan failed immediately on
`appGroupLeastPrivilegeRule.ceremonyCostIsZero` — *"**the two App Group records**
are allocated at that sitting"* — which **neither the reviewer nor r5 had
spotted**. Corrected, and the sentence now carries a note recording how it was
found.

### One deliberate divergence from the required rework, stated plainly

Rework item 1 asks the sentence to say the group is granted to the iOS host and
provider only and that every macOS target uses no custom group. The corrected
sentence states the exclusion **universally** — *"and to NO macOS target on either
channel, in either family (rules K1 and K2)"* — rather than naming the macOS host
as an excluded target.

Reason: **naming a target is exactly what went stale.** r4 named the host as a
grantee, r5 moved the row, the name stayed. A universal clause is derived from all
eight rows by R32 and has nothing to forget. The host-specific reasoning already
lives in rule K2 and §4.4 D-8. The reviewer's gate — which asserts textually that
this sentence no longer contains "macOS host" — **passes**. This is recorded in
the JSON as `whyTheMacOSHostIsNotNamed` rather than left as an unexplained
difference from the instruction.

---

## 2. F2 — the portal mutation task still carried the pre-r5 capability contract

**CONFIRMED.** `TASK-260715-3jloqy`'s board record was read in full; all four
quoted statements were present verbatim. Its dependency edges confirm the
sequencing the finding assumes — blocked by this matrix, by `TASK-260715-apc34w`
and by `TASK-260728-q5kjta` — so it runs **after** Ceremony C1, unattended, with
whatever its own board record says. r5 corrected the *human* node and left the node
that *spends* the authorization untouched.

### The worst of the four statements is not an App Groups regression

AC2 required the packet-tunnel entitlement **only on provider identifiers**. This
contract's own target rows have required
`com.apple.developer.networking.networkextension` on **all eight** targets since
r1, and **R7 has enforced it since r2**. So that clause contradicted r1 as much as
it contradicts r6: provider-only verification would have *passed* a portal state in
which neither macOS host App ID carries the capability, and the failure would have
surfaced at first build, after the sitting was over. Checked independently of
anything r5 changed.

### The fix

`TASK-260715-3jloqy`'s **description, scope, acceptance criteria and checklist**
are corrected on the board to `c1AuthorizationScope` exactly:

- four named macOS App IDs — `works.relux.tunnel.mac`,
  `works.relux.tunnel.mac.tunnel`, `works.relux.tunnel.probe.mac`,
  `works.relux.tunnel.probe.mac.tunnel`;
- **Network Extensions enabled on all four, hosts included**;
- no App Group record and no App Groups capability;
- no Keychain access-group portal mutation (there is no such portal record);
- one Mac Development profile per App ID with this Mac registered;
- all four iOS App IDs and every distribution profile explicitly excluded;
- profile inspection enforcing the unsuffixed `packet-tunnel-provider` value on
  **host and provider profiles alike**.

Four checklist items were added for the specifics the generic three did not cover.
The board DSL has no checklist-item edit or delete, so the original three are
retained; none of them contradicts r6.

The requirement is pinned in the JSON as `portalMutationTaskContract` (**rule
P1**) — required mutations, forbidden mutations, the two negative assertions, and
the three exact pre-r5 phrases that may never return — and
**`TASK-260715-ypo7yo_check-portal-consumer.py`** reads the pin plus the live
board record and exits non-zero on drift. R32 checks that the pin still agrees
with `c1AuthorizationScope`; the script checks that the board still agrees with the
pin. The reviewer's hand check is now reproducible.

