LOGBOOK.md:27:- FINDING: r1–r4 left all four **probe** keychain rows `prohibited` with **no reopening condition** — settled by *omission*, indistinguishable from an oversight and reopenable by anyone reading the silence as "nobody considered it". Found by the new R31 gate on its first run, not by inspection. Rows fixed, rule kept.
LOGBOOK.md:29:- SCOPE: `TASK-260728-q5kjta` (Ceremony C1) scope + AC4 corrected on the board (verdict 04 F2): four macOS App IDs not a singular pair, Network Extensions only, **zero** App Group records/capabilities, **no** Keychain mutation (there is no portal record), Mac Development profile class, all four iOS App IDs excluded. A downstream-consequence paragraph in the matrix is not the contract the operator reads.
LOGBOOK.md:30:- VERIFICATION: **846 checks** exit 0 (r4: 801); **92 negative gates** exit 0 (r4: 66), 26 new — 14 attacking F1 including the over-correction (an iOS row withheld must also fail) and re-granting *with* a laundered consumer entry, 10 attacking F3, 2 deleting assertions A17/A18. **32/32 r4 preservation assertions hold**, run against a pre-edit copy of the r4 JSON rather than argued from prose. `check-legacy-preservation.sh`, `task-board validate`, `swift test` (335 tests / 29 suites) all exit 0. Both reviewer `jq` gates flipped `false`/exit 1 → `true`/exit 0.
LOGBOOK.md:32:### 0912 — "Retained for a channel a future task will select" is a grant with no function; the sandbox profile settles what an entitlement is actually for (TASK-260715-ypo7yo, r4)
LOGBOOK.md:33:- ROOT CAUSE: r3 granted `com.apple.security.application-groups` on both macOS production targets with the purpose *"the sandbox-visible shared namespace for the host↔provider channel selected by `TASK-260728-7ii1xz` **will** use"*, plus "so C1 need not be repeated". Reviewer verdict 03 blocked it: a possible future transport plus an operational convenience are not a current requirement. Both halves confirmed against the r3 artifact. The generalisable failure is **holding a capability open against an undecided design** — it reads as prudent and is indistinguishable from over-provisioning until the design lands.
LOGBOOK.md:34:- DECISION: `application-groups` is now **prohibited on all four macOS targets** (production *and* probe) and `required` on all four iOS targets — new rule **G4**: an App Group is granted only where a *currently selected* mechanism uses one of the grants the entitlement actually confers. Reopening conditions are deliberately **grant-specific** (group-prefixed Mach service / group-prefixed POSIX IPC / provider moved into a user context), so "the spec mentions App Groups" or "a shipping product carries it" cannot reopen the row.
LOGBOOK.md:35:- FINDING: What the entitlement grants on macOS is readable, not arguable. `/System/Library/Sandbox/Profiles/application.sb` (26.5/25F71) contains exactly two `sandbox-array-entitlement` blocks for the key — lines **302-330** and **459-465** — conferring six grants. Three are **home-relative** and therefore break across the root/user split (group container r/w; `network-bind`/`network-outbound` inside it; `~/Library/Application Scripts/<suite>`). Three are **system-wide and do work across it**: `/Library/Application Support/AppStore/GroupContent/<suite>`, `mach-lookup`/`mach-register` on `global-name-prefix "<suite>."` (**lines 326-328** — the XPC channel, and the only reason the macOS group ever looked load-bearing), and `ipc-posix*` on `ipc-posix-name-prefix "<suite>/"` (**line 329** — POSIX shm, which no candidate ever proposed). "App Groups do nothing on macOS" would be the tidy version and it is false; the verdict rests on *these grants are unused*, not on *the entitlement is inert*.
LOGBOOK.md:36:- FINDING: A **private** group container is redundant, not merely unused. `(appsandbox-container-common)` / `(appsandbox-container-macos)` at `application.sb:107-108` sit at top level, inside no `(when (entitlement …))` form — every sandboxed target already has its own container from `application_container_id`, unentitled. The only thing a group container adds is *sharing*. Same for grant 2: `application.sb:754-756` already allows bind/connect on the target's own container. This answers the one question `7ii1xz` M6 left open, and answers it harder than M6 expected.
LOGBOOK.md:37:- FINDING: OC-4 gained evidence while being downgraded to dormant. `application.sb:326-328` builds the authorized Mach prefix as `(global-name-prefix (string-append suite "."))` from the **literal entitlement value**, whatever its style — so on the sandbox side an iOS-style `group.` ID would work as a prefix, contradicting r3's flat "no source addresses it". Not proof: the `launchd` bootstrap and profile-authorization sides were not exercised and nothing was signed.
LOGBOOK.md:38:- DECISION: **Ceremony C1 shrank.** No App Group record is created and the App Groups capability is enabled on no App ID. The convenience argument did not need weighing — it evaporates: C1's `explicitlyNotAuthorized` already excludes all four iOS App IDs under ADR-024, so iOS provisioning *already* needs a later portal sitting, and those four iOS App IDs are the records' only consumers. New rule **R30** makes C1 scope **derived** from what its App IDs require, so growing or shrinking it by hand cannot pass.
LOGBOOK.md:40:- SCOPE: `PlatformVPNIdentity` (`Sources/ReluxTunnelCore/VPNManagerRepository.swift:17`) declares `appGroupIdentifier` as a non-optional `String` for both platforms — after G4 there is no macOS value to supply. Recorded for `TASK-260715-1tzaed`, not fixed; `production(for:)` still throws so nothing is broken today. `.spec/architecture.md:111` joins `security-claims.md` and threat-model M-02 on the spec-amendment list.
LOGBOOK.md:41:- VERIFICATION: 30 rules / **801 checks** exit 0 (r3: 598/R1-R27); **66 negative gates** exit 0 (r3: 41), 25 new — 15 attacking F1 including the **over-correction** (an iOS row withheld must also fail) and "the same defect one row over" (the probe), 9 attacking the `7ii1xz` amendment packet, 1 deleting assertion A16. 15/15 r3 preservation assertions hold. `check-legacy-preservation.sh`, `task-board validate`, `swift test` (335 tests / 29 suites) all exit 0. Reviewer's own targeted `jq` gate flipped `false`/exit 1 → `true`/exit 0.
LOGBOOK.md:45:- DECISION: Revocation is now specified as a **five-state table**, not a yes/no. Synchronous when the configuration is installed and enabled (running or not — the send launches it); unavailable and deferred to a start-time sweep when the configuration is disabled (`NEVPNErrorConfigurationDisabled`), invalid/unapproved (`NEVPNErrorConfigurationInvalid`), or when no host is running at all. Seeding moved out of `startTunnel` entirely, so the credential is normally at rest before any start; `startTunnel` is now read-only and fails fast with `credentialNotProvisioned`. The start-time sweep survives as crash-recovery defence, not as the deletion path.
LOGBOOK.md:47:- FINDING: `kSecUseKeychain` is **add-only**. `SecItem.h` defines it as "a value of type `SecKeychainRef` to which **`SecItemAdd`** will add the provided item(s)"; scoping a *search* takes `kSecMatchSearchList`. r1 specified `kSecUseKeychain` for the query. Verified end to end against a throwaway keychain — add/read/delete all `OSStatus=0`, and a **negative control** with the search list pointed at the user default returns `-25300 errSecItemNotFound`, proving the list actually scopes rather than being silently ignored. This also gives `379cpk`/`1o9wjz` a real unit-test seam needing neither root nor the System keychain.
LOGBOOK.md:48:- FINDING: Do not hard-code `/Library/Keychains/System.keychain`. `SecKeychainCopyDomainDefault(kSecPreferencesDomainSystem, &kc)` returns `OSStatus=0` and resolves to exactly that path from an ordinary user context (uid 502), and `SecKeychainCopyDomainSearchList` for the system domain has **count=1** — physically confirming TN3137's *"in the system context the search list includes just the System keychain"*. The fix is behaviour-preserving, so there was never a reason to pin the literal. The whole `SecKeychain`/`SecAccess` surface is `API_DEPRECATED(… macos(10.2, 10.10))` yet still ships in the 26.5 SDK and remains Apple's only documented daemon option.
LOGBOOK.md:49:- ANOMALY: `Security.framework/Headers/SecKeychain.h` is **ISO-8859 encoded**. A plain UTF-8 `grep` over it silently prints nothing and exits 1 — indistinguishable from "the symbol isn't there". This nearly produced the opposite error, concluding the domain APIs had been removed from the SDK. `iconv -f ISO-8859-1 -t UTF-8` first when grepping Apple headers; an empty grep result on a header is not evidence of absence.
LOGBOOK.md:50:- DECISION: A settled decision row must have its **pending scaffolding deleted with it**. The ypo7yo matrix row still carried `resolutionOwner: TASK-260728-7ii1xz` and an `amendmentRule` reading "if the transport gives the provider direct keychain access, amend to `required` with the group literal". The selected transport makes that antecedent **true** while the file-based keychain makes the consequent **false** — so leaving it in place was a live instruction to re-grant the row on precisely the reasoning the decision refutes. The amendment packet now covers all five keys on the row field by field. Worth a validator gate: a `prohibited` row that still names a `resolutionOwner` is a contradiction.
LOGBOOK.md:52:### 0822 — macOS credential transport decided: the root provider owns a System keychain item, seeded once over the NE app-message channel (TASK-260728-7ii1xz)
LOGBOOK.md:53:- DECISION: Candidate D selected. Host reads the secret in user context, hands it to the running provider via `sendProviderMessage` → `handleAppMessage`, and the **root provider** persists it in the file-based **System keychain** (`/Library/Keychains/System.keychain`) with a restrictive `SecAccess`. This is exactly Apple DTS's recommendation for this shape (forums/thread/759976, /133933). Rejected: shared Data Protection Keychain group (cannot work), memory-only IPC seed (dies on system start), custom XPC Mach service (needs exception entitlements + makes OC-4 live), root-owned encrypted file (Tailscale's pattern — same security, more code, more entitlements).
LOGBOOK.md:54:- FINDING: The decisive fact is physically readable on this Mac. `/System/Library/Sandbox/Profiles/application.sb:792-793` (macOS 26.5/25F71, sha256 `2e711078…`): `(when (entitlement "com.apple.developer.networking.networkextension") (allow file-read* file-write* (subpath "/Library/Keychains")))`. An NE-entitled process already has System keychain access from the base App Sandbox. Lines 655-656 grant the `securityd`/`SecurityServer` Mach lookups to every sandboxed process. **The selected transport costs zero entitlements.**
LOGBOOK.md:55:- DECISION: `keychain-access-groups` on `works.relux.tunnel.mac.tunnel` — **keep prohibited**, promoted from `prohibited-pending-decision` to settled. Not "unnecessary" but *inapplicable*: the file-based keychain is ACL-controlled (`SecAccess`), access groups are a Data Protection Keychain concept only (TN3137). Applying owner is `TASK-260715-ypo7yo`; closes OC-1, downgrades OC-4.
LOGBOOK.md:56:- ANOMALY: Second instance of the pattern logged at 0755 — **shipping entitlements are not evidence of requirement**. Surfshark's provider ships a `/Library/Keychains/` temporary exception the current sandbox profile makes redundant; Surfshark and Tailscale both ship `/private/var/db/mds/` exceptions dating to a Catalina-era bug fixed in Big Sur (forums/thread/660123), which `application.sb:544-546` already covers for uid 0; and PureVPN's provider carries `keychain-access-groups` that Facts 1-2 say cannot bridge the user boundary. Three shipping products, three stale or inert entitlements.
LOGBOOK.md:57:- FINDING: **The App Group channel is broken on macOS too**, not just the keychain. `containerURL(forSecurityApplicationGroupIdentifier:)` resolves relative to the caller's home and the root provider's home is `/private/var/root` (verified root-private, `ls` exit 1). `.spec/architecture.md:111` says shared state is "an App Group configuration snapshot and Keychain references" — both halves fail on macOS. The non-secret snapshot must travel in `providerConfiguration`, which the accepted M1 lifecycle contract already does. Directly affects `TASK-260715-3f4lxy`.
LOGBOOK.md:58:- FINDING: One property macOS **cannot** offer that iOS does: the System keychain unlocks at boot from root-only `/private/var/db/SystemKey`, so the secret is **not** protected by the user's login password. ~~(2) deletion cannot be synchronous — the user-context app cannot delete a root-owned item while the provider is stopped, so revocation is a provider request reconciled by a start-time sweep.~~ **CORRECTED in r2 — see the 0851 entry.** Revocation *is* synchronous whenever the NE configuration is installed and enabled; the r1 claim rested on a misread SDK header.
LOGBOOK.md:59:- FINDING: System keychain **attributes are world-readable** — `security dump-keychain /Library/Keychains/System.keychain` as uid 502 exits 0 and prints 192 items' attributes. Item *data* is ACL-gated (`-d` blocked on an interactive authorization prompt, killed at 120s). Consequence: the credential item's service/account values must carry no hostname, account, or profile name.
LOGBOOK.md:60:- NOTE: Experiment design worth reusing. `ssh localhost` gives a **`Background`** launchd session at the same uid while the terminal is **`Aqua`** — a free, no-root way to isolate the "user login context" axis. It showed the file-based login keychain returning `OSStatus=0` in Aqua and `-25308 errSecInteractionNotAllowed` in Background. It could **not** test the Data Protection Keychain: an ad-hoc-signed CLI returns `-34018 errSecMissingEntitlement` in both contexts, because DP access groups must be authorized by a provisioning profile (TN3137) and Ceremony C1 has not run.
LOGBOOK.md:61:- BLOCKED: Nothing. Named limitation instead — `sudo -n true` exit 1 in a non-interactive session, so **no code was run as root**. Checks V1–V3 (V1 System keychain write from the signed sandboxed provider, V2 root group-container path, V3 system start with the app not running) were appended to `TASK-260715-9yp8to` rather than creating a duplicate physical-Mac node (ADR-028a). r2 added **V4/V5** to the same node.
LOGBOOK.md:63:### 0755 — An exhaustive entitlement allowlist inherits every entitlement the architecture implies, including ones the matrix only mentioned in prose (TASK-260715-ypo7yo, r3)
LOGBOOK.md:64:- ROOT CAUSE: r2 argued in D-4 that `ReluxProxyMac` is sandboxed *because of* Sparkle, and quoted the spec clause naming the `-spks`/`-spki` Mach lookup exceptions — then never authored `com.apple.security.temporary-exception.mach-lookup.global-name` as an entitlement row. The r2 containment assertion A10b declares every present key must be authored or signing-generated, so the allowlist would have **rejected a correctly built sandboxed Sparkle host**. Same defect class as r1's F3, one revision later: prose acknowledged the requirement, the machine-checkable set did not. The generalisable lesson is that the moment an assertion becomes *exhaustive*, every architectural consequence stated anywhere in the contract must also appear as data — rationale text is no longer a place a requirement can live.
LOGBOOK.md:65:- FINDING: Verified three ways before accepting. `.spec/platform-distribution.md:103-105` mandates the two exceptions; this repo's own `.research/260721_macos-self-update.md:111-113` records the identical two-value set independently; and `codesign -d --entitlements :-` across `/Applications` found six shipping apps carrying exactly the `<bundle-id>-spks` / `-spki` pattern, three of them (Mimestream, WhatsApp, Slipbox) sandboxed with those two values and nothing else.
LOGBOOK.md:66:- ANOMALY: **Tailscale carries the Sparkle exception while `com.apple.security.app-sandbox` is `false`** — the same product cited as D-4 evidence that hosts are routinely unsandboxed. The exception is inert without the sandbox, so "shipping products carry this key" is not by itself evidence that a key is required. Worth remembering when mining installed apps for entitlement precedent: presence proves the shape is *accepted*, never that it is *needed*.
LOGBOOK.md:68:- DECISION: The row is attributed `project-architecture`, **not** `sandbox-consequence`, even though `com.apple.security.network.client` on the same target is a sandbox consequence and is equally Sparkle-driven. `network.client` follows mechanically from sandboxing alone; this key needs two independent project decisions to both hold (ADR-018's Sparkle choice **and** the host sandbox), so it is not derivable from "this target is sandboxed". Also added **rule X1**: the spec bans "unreviewed exception entitlement", so the contract now carries a reviewed-exception register plus an explicit banned set for Hardened Runtime relaxations — `get-task-allow` deliberately excluded from that set, since it is toolchain-injected on the development channel and governed by A10d instead. Without X1 this defect recurs one key at a time.
LOGBOOK.md:71:### 0730 — Entitlement values are a property of the signing channel, not of the target (TASK-260715-ypo7yo, r2)
LOGBOOK.md:72:- ROOT CAUSE: The r1 matrix assigned `packet-tunnel-provider-systemextension` to all four macOS targets unconditionally while authorizing **Mac Development** profiles for the same four App IDs. Apple DTS is explicit that the value follows the distribution channel: the `-systemextension` suffix is for direct Developer ID signing, and "otherwise — including when you distribute the app on the App Store and when signing for development — use the values without that suffix" (forums/thread/800887). Ceremony C1 would have provisioned development profiles against a value development signing never uses, and C1 is a one-shot human node (ADR-028).
LOGBOOK.md:73:- FINDING: Verified against three shipping Developer-ID packet-tunnel products installed on this Mac — Tailscale, PureVPN, Surfshark. All three carry the suffixed value on **both** the containing app and the embedded sysex, confirming the Developer ID half. No development-signed counterpart exists locally, so the development half rests on the DTS statement and is re-checked by assertion A3 at first signed build.
LOGBOOK.md:74:- DECISION: Every NE value in the contract is now keyed by channel (`development` / `developer-id` / `app-store`), each profile row records `authorizesNetworkExtensionValues`, and the C1 scope is pinned to development-channel values so the ceremony cannot be pointed at the wrong one. Consequence worth remembering: the probe family is development-signed only, so it can prove the **development** entitlement shape and nothing else — r1 implied it proved the production shape.
LOGBOOK.md:81:### 0722 — iOS-style App Group IDs are the correct macOS choice since Feb 2025, with one unverified corner (TASK-260715-ypo7yo, r2)
LOGBOOK.md:82:- ROOT CAUSE: r1 registered `group.works.relux.tunnel` but had macOS claim `262RZ595FP.group.works.relux.tunnel`, calling it "the same record with a team prefix". Those are two different Apple identifier *styles*, not two spellings of one record: iOS-style begins `group.` and is allocated on the Developer website; macOS-style begins with the Team ID and **cannot** be allocated there at all. The macOS literal therefore did not identify the portal record the contract said it consumed.
LOGBOOK.md:83:- FINDING: Apple completed Developer-website support for iOS-style app group IDs on macOS on **21 Feb 2025**, and Xcode 16.3+ makes it the macOS default (forums/thread/721701). This project is new on Xcode 26.5, so both platforms now claim the identical registered literal.
LOGBOOK.md:84:- ANOMALY: Every Apple example of an app group used as a sandboxed **Mach service name prefix**, and all three shipping products inspected here, use the macOS-style Team-ID form — and all predate Feb 2025. No source found confirms an iOS-style ID works as a Mach service prefix. Not blocking: the spec's host↔provider channel is an App Group snapshot plus Keychain references (`.spec/architecture.md:111`), not XPC. Recorded as **OC-4** owned by `TASK-260728-7ii1xz`, with the amendment path being a separate *unregistered* macOS-style ID for that one purpose. This supersedes the 0657 claim below that the macOS group is required "as the XPC Mach-service namespace" — that purpose is now conditional, not established.
LOGBOOK.md:85:- FINDING: The r1 D-2 rationale ("the entitlement would be non-functional") was too strong. PureVPN's root sysexes carry `keychain-access-groups` outright and Surfshark's carries a `/Library/Keychains/` path exception — i.e. the **file-based System keychain** works from root. What fails is specifically the *Data Protection* Keychain sharing the spec asks for. The row stays withheld because its purpose is undetermined until `7ii1xz` picks a transport, not because it is inert.
LOGBOOK.md:87:### 0718 — A containment assertion that ignores signing-generated entitlements is unrunnable (TASK-260715-ypo7yo, r2)
LOGBOOK.md:88:- ROOT CAUSE: r1's assertion A10 rejected "any entitlement key outside the matrix union", but the matrix authors only project capabilities. The signing toolchain injects more, so A10 would have failed a legitimately signed development bundle — or forced undocumented local exceptions, which is worse.
LOGBOOK.md:89:- FINDING: Established empirically with `codesign -d --entitlements :-` over four signed macOS bundles and `security cms -D` over a local Mac Development profile. macOS always gets `com.apple.application-identifier` and `com.apple.developer.team-identifier`; debug builds additionally get `com.apple.security.get-task-allow`, stripped on distribution export. Note the macOS spellings carry the `com.apple.` prefix where iOS does not. A Mac Development profile authorizes `keychain-access-groups` as a team-wide `<TEAMID>.*` wildcard, which is why the shared keychain group needs no portal record of its own.
LOGBOOK.md:90:- DECISION: A10 became four assertions over a computed allowlist — completeness, containment, exclusion, channel hygiene — where the allowlist is authored keys ∪ always-injected ∪ (development-only if that channel). iOS spellings are recorded at **medium confidence** with `3dno4w` named as verifier, since no iOS bundle was built here; guessing them silently would have been the same class of error.
LOGBOOK.md:91:- VERIFICATION: 24 rules / 537 checks over the JSON, exit 0, plus **23 negative gates** that each re-introduce a defect and must fail closed naming their rule (6 for the NE-channel defect, 4 App Group, 3 allowlist, 3 sandbox attribution, 7 re-checking preserved r1 decisions). The r1 harness mutated the JSON with regex substitution, which can silently no-op; r2 mutates the parsed object, so a mutation that fails to apply is itself reported as a gate failure.
LOGBOOK.md:93:### 0657 — The macOS provider runs as root, so the specified Keychain sharing cannot work (TASK-260715-ypo7yo)
LOGBOOK.md:94:- BLOCKER: `.spec/security-privacy.md` requires private keys and passphrases to live in the Data Protection Keychain "shared only through the minimum keychain access group required by the app and its packet tunnel extension" (also `.spec/threat-model.md` DF-02/M-02). On macOS that is unsatisfiable. Developer ID direct distribution forces the provider to be a **system extension** (TN3134, `.spec/platform-distribution.md`), a sysex runs **as root** outside a user context, the Data Protection Keychain is available only to code in a user context, and keychain access groups share items between programs running as the **same** user. The host runs as the logged-in user. iOS is unaffected — its appex runs as the same user as its host.
LOGBOOK.md:95:- FINDING: The same root/user split kills the App Group **container** as a macOS host↔provider channel: the sysex's container resolves under `/var/root/Library/Group Containers`, the host's under the user's home. App Groups are still **required** on macOS, but only as the XPC Mach-service namespace a sandboxed process must prefix with — not as shared storage. `TASK-260715-3f4lxy` (profile snapshot loader) inherits this.
LOGBOOK.md:96:- DECISION: The matrix withholds `keychain-access-groups` from `works.relux.tunnel.mac.tunnel` (`prohibited-pending-decision`) rather than provisioning a right the provider cannot exercise. Created `TASK-260728-7ii1xz` to select the macOS credential transport and return a grant-or-keep-prohibited amendment; it blocks `379cpk`, `1o9wjz`, `29ws8l`, `3f4lxy`. Five credential tasks all assumed the shared-group model and none owned the question of whether it holds.
LOGBOOK.md:97:- NOTE: The trap that made this invisible is that the entitlement is *accepted* everywhere — portal, profile, and `codesign` all succeed. Only a runtime `SecItem` call in the provider fails, which is M1-deep. Least privilege caught it because "grant only what the target can actually use" forced the question.
LOGBOOK.md:99:### 0653 — Target name is not bundle identifier, and that is a legacy-collision hazard (TASK-260715-ypo7yo)
LOGBOOK.md:100:- DECISION: Apple identifiers are `works.relux.tunnel.{mac,mac.tunnel,ios,ios.tunnel}` plus a disjoint disposable probe family `works.relux.tunnel.probe.*`, under Team `262RZ595FP` (Relux Works, LLC). Provider identifier is always host identifier + `.tunnel`, so containment is derivable in both directions with no lookup table.
LOGBOOK.md:101:- FINDING: The generated target *names* from `.spec/platform-distribution.md` are `ReluxProxyMac`/`ReluxProxyIOS`. Any generator that derives a bundle identifier from the target name emits `works.relux.proxy.mac` — inside the shipped legacy `works.relux.proxy` identity namespace, which is exactly the silent-repurposing failure AC4 forbids. Target name and bundle identifier are explicitly decoupled and assertion A11 (`^works\.relux\.proxy(\.|$)`) exists to catch it.
LOGBOOK.md:102:- DECISION: One bundle identifier per target across every build configuration — no `.debug`/`.dev` variants. macOS system-extension approval is keyed to Team ID + extension bundle identifier, so suffixing multiplies physical approval friction on Holds H2/H3, and `9yp8to` must exercise the identity that ships. Environment is expressed by signing identity class, profile class, hardened runtime, and `get-task-allow`. The single exception is the probe family, which exists so throwaway sysex approvals never burn the production identity.
LOGBOOK.md:103:- FINDING: Probe identifiers had to be in this matrix, not invented later by `1r0fxv`. C1 authorizes "exactly the approved macOS entries"; had the probe been absent, provisioning it would have needed a second human sitting — the thing ADR-028 forbids.
LOGBOOK.md:104:- VERIFICATION: `validate_matrix.py` runs 18 rules / 247 checks over the JSON contract, exit 0; nine deliberate mutations (broken containment, legacy-prefixed identifier, wrong NE value, granted pending-keychain row, unprefixed macOS group, collapsed probe group, C1-authorized distribution profile, iOS marked provisioned, sysex-install on the provider) each fail closed with the expected rule.
LOGBOOK.md:120:### 0742 — A human ceremony has to be one board node, or the graph re-splits it (TASK-260728-3a2dnr, round 3)
LOGBOOK.md:121:- ANOMALY: Round 2 described Ceremony C1 as one sitting, but no board element owned the sitting. The four grant-bearing tasks were ordered `apc34w` → {`3jloqy`, `dveo1o`}, so a `max_parallel = 1` scheduler produced **two** human stops with a full producer→reviewer cycle between them. Prose cannot batch a human interaction; only the DAG can. Recorded as ADR-028 and fixed by creating `TASK-260728-q5kjta`, the single up-front human-input node; `apc34w`, `3jloqy`, `dveo1o`, `ziprhs` are now unattended agent evidence tasks behind it, with their evidence obligations intact.
LOGBOOK.md:133:- DECISION: Ceremony C1 split. C1 is permission grants only (Keychain unlock, portal auth, identifier/profile authorization, notarytool profile, Sparkle keygen). The probe build `1r0fxv` and its independent review sit **outside** it, and Approval A1 (one click on the probe's system-VPN dialog) is a separate later interaction. The previous version claimed a human could remain present through a producer→reviewer cycle.
LOGBOOK.md:134:- FINDING: The notarization credential was described as "available" while existing only as a mode-0600 `.p8` file — the project invariant is Keychain-only. ADR-025 now requires a named `notarytool` Keychain profile, a real authentication check, and a recorded disposition for the source file; `TASK-260728-dveo1o` owns it and blocks `3gkwn0`.
LOGBOOK.md:135:- DECISION: Sparkle key generation separated from updater integration (ADR-026). `ziprhs` may run at C1 because it depends on nothing; `SUPublicEDKey` pinning and appcast sign/verify became `TASK-260728-3bj9bk`, blocked by `uyju7n` → `xempiv` and `1mt4e7`. Accepting the key ceremony no longer implies a working self-update signing path.
LOGBOOK.md:136:- FINDING: `TASK-260715-32umrc` (generated-project architecture ADR) is blocked by `2ayxqn` (Gate P0). That is why only 35 tasks precede the ceremony — an ADR-011 gate deliberately kept, not a graph defect. Schedule C1 early and run segment 1 alongside arranging it.
LOGBOOK.md:140:### 0612 — Board replanned to the macOS prototype path; 202 autonomous tasks behind one ceremony (TASK-260728-3a2dnr)
LOGBOOK.md:141:> Superseded by the 0655 entry: the 202-task claim, the single-session Ceremony C1, and the "notary credentials exist" readiness statement were all corrected.
LOGBOOK.md:146:- DECISION: Ceremony C1 deliberately includes the Gate P0 system-VPN approval click. The disposable probe builds in minutes from profiles created minutes earlier, so it stays one sitting. Hold H2 (real app, different bundle identifier) cannot be folded in and remains the next human boundary after 202 autonomous tasks.
LOGBOOK.md:148:- VERIFICATION: `task-board validate` → valid, 0 issues (was 46). Independent cycle traversal → 0. No source tree change: `git status --porcelain -- Sources Tests Protocol relay scripts config Package.swift Makefile` is empty. Invariant scan confirms host-key-before-auth, Keychain-only secrets, fail-closed DNS, bounded memory, public packetFlow bridge, rootless exec/stdio relay, and signed/notarized release all survive the edit. Evidence: `.task-board/.resources/TASK-260728-3a2dnr/`.
LOGBOOK.md:192:- REWORK: Manifest v1 now uses closed versioned structures with fixed units and required metric/counter families, explicit selected-SSH DNS limits, bundle-relative artifacts, authorization/custodian/redacted-derivative/retention fields, and schema conditionals that reject claimed passes with failed safety gates, unauthorized captures, or production authorization with blockers/provisional state.
LOGBOOK.md:193:- REPRODUCIBILITY: The protocol now fixes the RFC 8785 canonical row projection, identifier/seed relationships, SplitMix64 stream derivation, packet order and loss/jitter sampling, exact paired-effect integer rounding, 10,000-resample bootstrap index mapping, multiplicity group/coverage, quantiles, and inclusive equality/strict regression-boundary classification.
LOGBOOK.md:207:- CORRECTION: Sparkle 2 removed downgrade support, so emergency rollback is last-known-good source rebuilt at a higher `CFBundleVersion`, not an explicit rollback channel. Sparkle also documents Developer ID key rotation, so downstream tests must enforce the approved product identities instead of asserting every Team ID change is intrinsically rejected by Sparkle.
LOGBOOK.md:208:- PLATFORM: Apple TN3134 requires the directly distributed macOS packet tunnel to be a system extension. Sparkle host replacement/relaunch does not activate the new provider; the host must stop the tunnel, relaunch, submit a separate replacement request, surface approval/restart state, and avoid automatic reconnect. Exact OS behavior remains a physical gate under `TASK-260715-1r48pc` and `TASK-260715-2aessv`.
LOGBOOK.md:212:- FINDING: The seed threat model stated several accepted designs as shipped mitigations even though the repository has no concrete packet-tunnel provider target, selected production SSH adapter, Keychain vault, DNS/route composition, relay deployment/frame-pump composition, degraded-mode controller, platform fail-closed implementation, Sparkle path, or application release pipeline.
LOGBOOK.md:215:- RESIDUAL: Production SSH selection (`TASK-260715-1gjxer`), DNS runtime authorization (`TASK-260721-3miqh4`), physical route/DNS evidence, credentials/entitlements, relay composition, update/release controls, and independent candidate review remain explicit evidence gates.
LOGBOOK.md:223:- DECISION: `relux-relay` accepts only `--identity --protocol 1` and `--stdio --protocol 1`; every unknown, reordered, duplicated, missing, legacy, or oversized invocation exits 64 without echoing input. Fixed exits distinguish protocol rejection (65), internal identity/configuration failure (70), stream failure (74), and signal/cancellation (130).
LOGBOOK.md:229:- DECISION: `relay/toolchain-manifest-v1.json` is the checked-in authority for Go 1.26.5 `gc`/internal-linker identity, official Go and Syft host archive hashes, the standard-library-only module lock, license hashes/identifiers, four target/CPU baselines, the absence of build SDKs/sysroots, macOS 12.0, deterministic flags, and the exact actions/checkout v7.0.1 revision. Linux static linkage does not justify a kernel-version floor; the declared runtime baseline is native unprivileged Ubuntu 24.04 on both x86_64 and arm64 CI fixtures.
LOGBOOK.md:326:- GENERATION/PRIVACY: Runtime generation remains M1-owned, transport generation is M3-owned, and relay attempt/active generation is M2-owned. Association identity cannot cross that tuple. Schema-2 runtime/capability snapshots add finite reasons, retry owner, generations, and locally validated build/limit evidence while preserving explicit schema-1 responses; remote text and sensitive traffic identifiers remain structurally excluded.
LOGBOOK.md:332:- ARTIFACT CONTRACT: The Apple bundle input contains the accepted four relay names, strict manifest-v1 JSON, sorted SHA-256 file, four SPDX 2.3 Syft SBOMs, and combined repository/Go notices. Verification rejects unknown/missing files or fields, noncanonical paths/names, wrong architecture/build metadata, unreviewed dependencies, wrong Go license provenance, host paths, dirty release checkouts, and source-revision mismatch.
LOGBOOK.md:333:- PROVENANCE REWORK: Release entry points now require offline-provisioned official Go and Syft archives retained beside their installed tools. They recheck the accepted host-specific archive hash, canonical path-free receipt, installed archive members, exact Go 1.26.5 host identity with `GOTOOLCHAIN=local`, and Syft 1.48.0 commit `3e2bc6ed095f7ec1a415fb38cfe1c319e95dfed6`; missing, older/newer, wrong-platform, wrong-commit, wrong-checksum, auto-toolchain, and version-only inputs fail closed.
LOGBOOK.md:337:- PRIVACY: The board runner's automatically attached raw spawn log was removed from the versionable outcome resources. Persistent evidence contains no raw process log, host identifier, absolute local path, credential, payload, or remote-controlled string.
LOGBOOK.md:342:- ARCHIVE ANOMALY / FIX: The first macOS tar pass added 15 AppleDouble `._*` metadata members. The published bundle is rebuilt with metadata copying disabled and is now exactly 15 declared source members; byte comparisons, privacy scans, and raw-spawn-log exclusion pass for every member.
LOGBOOK.md:355:- PRIVACY INCIDENT: An early live environment-inspection output included local device/account identifiers. No identifier was copied into research, raw measurement, evidence-bundle, board-note, or downstream artifacts; the automatically retained runner log was sanitized to generic redaction markers before outcome publication. Future environment rows for this task are limited to hardware model, architecture, OS, tool version, and source revision.
LOGBOOK.md:361:- SAFETY/PLANNING: Resolver/policy validation and SSH `direct-tcpip` readiness precede route/DNS settings. Post-settings ordinary queries have only the virtual ingress -> authenticated SSH/relay -> explicit endpoint path. Endpoint exhaustion returns bounded SERVFAIL, withdraws safe-DNS capability, and clears settings; it cannot call the device resolver. Missing legacy resolver data yields `requiresResolverConfiguration` with no inferred migration. TASK-260721-33o8fc owns schema/codec/migration, TASK-260721-2raag7 owns M4 profile experience, and revised TASK-260715-5o6jqg/TASK-260715-28jdml/TASK-260715-336ljl own shared-failure, M2, and integration evidence.
LOGBOOK.md:365:- DECISION: `TCPAdmissionRegistry` is one generation-scoped mutex authority for pending handshakes, authenticated flows, concurrent channel opens, owned channels, fixed directional queue capacity, live buffered bytes, and phase counts. All ceilings and the measured-safe/HEV evidence gates are caller-injected; denial is synchronous, retains no waiter, maps to SOCKS5 `REP=01`, and explicitly prohibits an SSH open. Reservation identifiers are monotonically allocated without reuse and fail closed with finite `identifier_capacity` pressure before `UInt64` wrap.
LOGBOOK.md:385:- RESOURCE: Adapter capacity is an atomic non-waiting reservation bounded by the HEV session ceiling and the measured M0/SSH/Core memory equation; exact flow, chunk, queue, window, and timeout values remain injected measurement inputs for M1/M3 rather than promoted constants. Diagnostics are aggregate schema-v1 only and exclude credentials, SOCKS bytes, endpoints, hostnames, payloads, and flow identifiers.
LOGBOOK.md:427:- IDENTITY: The complete host/provider/App Group/Keychain `PlatformVPNIdentity` remains injectable for deterministic fixtures. Both production factories fail closed until `TASK-260715-ypo7yo` supplies accepted signed bindings; no guessed identifier landed.
LOGBOOK.md:434:- SCHEMA: Provider stop metadata extends diagnostics v1 to 97 counters, 57 gauges, and 11 finite error codes across the same 10 domains. The schema still retains no endpoints, DNS names, traffic identifiers, secrets, configuration, localized errors, or free-form labels.
LOGBOOK.md:438:- DECISION: A manager is owned only when its protocol is `NETunnelProviderProtocol`, its provider bundle ID exactly matches the accepted platform binding, and its stable provider-configuration marker matches. Labels, server address, enabled state, profile UUID, schema version, and array order never establish ownership. Every write/remove rechecks a freshly loaded object; unmarked lookalikes and duplicates are zero-write conflicts outside explicit inactive duplicate repair.
LOGBOOK.md:441:- RESIDUAL: Exact four-target bundle IDs, App Group, Keychain group, and entitlements are not yet accepted (`TASK-260715-ypo7yo`). Shared implementation proceeds through injected identities, while production composition fails closed instead of inventing values. Human ratification stays decoupled in `TASK-260717-1dsqnj`.
LOGBOOK.md:490:- DECISION: M1 app messages use sorted-key protocol/schema v1 JSON with per-family 4/16/64 KiB limits and a strict bounded preflight that rejects invalid UTF-8, duplicate keys (including escape-equivalent keys), excessive nesting, corrupt numbers, trailing bytes, unsupported versions/kinds/values, and missing required fields before model use. The legacy `version` request/response remains the exact two-field v1 exception.
LOGBOOK.md:493:- VERIFICATION: `make validate-core` passes boundary/native-package checks, 182 Swift tests in 21 suites, and a post-test build; repository-wide Swift format lint is clean. Tests cover every model round-trip, golden bytes, additive fields, old/future versions, hostile/corrupt/oversize input, secret-shape regression, redacted finite errors, legacy compatibility, and both platform roots.
LOGBOOK.md:496:- DECISION: The packet-tunnel extension owns one actor-serialized runtime generation, with immutable app-message snapshots and no live forwarding dependency on the containing app. Startup is configuration -> verified/authenticated SSH -> TCP/DNS readiness -> pure packet preflight -> settings apply -> existing PacketBridge.start/read activation -> TCP + safe-DNS publication; every failure rolls back through one idempotent, non-cancellable stopping path.
LOGBOOK.md:499:- SAFETY: Settings clear is called only after a committed or uncertain apply. A required failed/uncertain clear publishes capabilities false but truthfully retains routesInstalled=true with routeState=clearFailed; a clear that was unnecessary or confirmed publishes routesInstalled=false. Legacy version protocol v1 remains byte-exact while new M1 commands/snapshots use explicit schemas.
LOGBOOK.md:698:- IMPLEMENTATION: Client KEX/host-key allowlists filter only bundled upstream implementations and fail closed when empty/unsupported. `NIOSSHHandler.negotiatedAlgorithmsSnapshot` copies exact KEX, host-key, cipher, and MAC strings from `NegotiationResult`; it never echoes configured lists.
LOGBOOK.md:709:- IMPLEMENTATION: Added `relay-portable-assets` / `inspect-assets`, which requires a caller-owned total bundle budget and emits a path-free four-asset report only after exact layout/name, regular executable mode, raw format/machine, Go CPU metadata, linkage, minimum runtime, dynamic-library, debug-section, size, SHA-256, and privacy checks. An over-budget report is retained before the command fails; no default budget silently creates product policy.
LOGBOOK.md:713:- DECISION NEEDED: Supply the approved total bundle budget in bytes, restore an approved native Linux fixture (the declared GitHub Actions rows after billing is fixed), and supply native Intel execution or explicitly approve Rosetta as the darwin/amd64 baseline. Until those inputs exist, AC3/AC4 cannot be claimed and the task remains blocked rather than treating cross-build or a one-byte diagnostic budget as release evidence.
LOGBOOK.md:808:- DECISION: iOS and macOS adapters share one locked `PacketFlowAdapterBoundary`; callback, task cancellation, and terminal adapter shutdown race for one continuation, while the callback registration remains a payload-free tombstone until NetworkExtension invokes it because `NEPacketTunnelFlow` exposes no deregistration API.
.spec/decisions.md:5:| ADR-001 | Accepted | Build a system VPN around `NEPacketTunnelProvider`; keep the live SSH transport inside the extension |
.spec/decisions.md:7:| ADR-003 | Accepted | Bridge `NEPacketTunnelFlow` through a public nonblocking `AF_UNIX/SOCK_DGRAM` socket pair; do not discover the utun FD |
.spec/decisions.md:17:| ADR-013 | Gate (release path) | Obtain evidence that local TCP termination + remote `direct-tcpip` is acceptable under Apple's packet-tunnel intended-use guidance. Re-scoped 2026-07-28: Gate A0 is an App Store/App Review release gate and is **not** a gate for the macOS prototype critical path. It stays mandatory before iOS submission and before public macOS distribution claims that depend on it. |
.spec/decisions.md:20:| ADR-016 | Accepted | Set new Apple targets to iOS 18.0 and macOS 15.0; preserve the legacy SwiftPM product at macOS 14.0; require minimum/current CI plus named physical baselines per TASK-260715-3r0993 |
.spec/decisions.md:22:| ADR-018 | Accepted | Pin Sparkle 2.9.4 exactly in `ReluxProxyMac`; require EdDSA-signed payloads plus a signed appcast with pre-extraction verification and no failure expiry; publish only final Developer ID-signed, hardened-runtime, notarized/stapled DMGs; use default stable plus opt-in `prerelease` items on the public HTTPS feed; replace/activate the direct-distribution packet-tunnel system extension only after host relaunch; use higher-build forward rollback because Sparkle 2 has no downgrade path; iOS stays with the App Store |
.spec/decisions.md:29:| ADR-024 | Accepted | The prototype physical target is **macOS-only on the current Apple-silicon Mac**. iOS targets, provider, UI, TestFlight, and App Store work are deferred; their identifiers stay defined in the Apple identifier/entitlement matrix so they resume without renaming. Gate P0 is recorded from macOS evidence alone. A Linux CI runner and native Linux relay-asset execution evidence are **not** required for the working-client path; they move to the release path (`TASK-260715-1c4l9v`). Relux Works Apple Development and Developer ID Application **signing identities** are present in the local Keychain. Notarization credentials are **not** yet in a usable Keychain form — see ADR-025. Secrets stay in the Keychain and are never copied to repo, board, logs, or `providerConfiguration`. |
.spec/decisions.md:30:| ADR-025 | Accepted | **Notarization credential custody is Keychain-only.** The only sanctioned runtime form of the App Store Connect notarization credential is a **named `notarytool` Keychain credential profile**; only that profile *name* may be recorded on the board, in the repo, or in CI configuration. The owner-supplied source `.p8` private key currently exists as a mode-0600 file on this host, which does not satisfy the Keychain-only invariant. Before the notarization path is declared ready, `TASK-260728-dveo1o` must (a) store the named profile, (b) verify it against the real notary service, and (c) record an explicit approved disposition for the source file — migration into Keychain-backed custody, retention under a named custodian with documented access control, or secure destruction. The file path, key ID, issuer ID, and key bytes are never echoed, logged, committed, or stored in board resources, and `set -x`/shell history must not capture the `store-credentials` invocation. CI keeps its own separately-scoped credential; the local profile is not exported. |
.spec/decisions.md:31:| ADR-026 | Accepted | **Sparkle key generation is separated from updater integration.** The EdDSA keypair is generated and taken into custody at the up-front ceremony `TASK-260728-q5kjta`, because it depends on nothing; `TASK-260717-ziprhs` records the resulting public-key/custody evidence unattended. `SUPublicEDKey` pinning, CI signing-secret binding, and appcast sign/verify evidence are a distinct downstream task (`TASK-260728-3bj9bk`) blocked by the generated macOS target (`TASK-260715-uyju7n` → `TASK-260717-xempiv`) and the appcast pipeline (`TASK-260717-1mt4e7`). An accepted key ceremony never implies a working self-update signing path. |
.spec/decisions.md:33:| ADR-028 | Accepted | **One human interaction is one board node, and a human node is never counted as autonomous.** (a) *Ownership.* A human sitting is owned by a single task; `TASK-260728-q5kjta` owns Ceremony C1 and is the only up-front human-input node. The evidence tasks it unblocks (`TASK-260715-apc34w`, `TASK-260715-3jloqy`, `TASK-260728-dveo1o`, `TASK-260717-ziprhs`) are agent work that runs unattended with the granted access and keeps its full evidence obligations. Spreading one sitting across several tasks forces the human to re-appear after each producer-reviewer cycle, which is what the round-2 review rejected. (b) *Labelling.* A task whose completion requires a human action or decision is a **human node** — a scheduler barrier that is never counted in an autonomous total, even when agent work follows inside the same node. Tasks that merely consume access or a decision granted earlier are autonomous and are reported as that node's follow-up. (c) *An approval that acknowledges a not-yet-existing artifact cannot be pre-granted*: Approval A1 (`TASK-260715-9yp8to`), sign-off S1 (`TASK-260715-2ayxqn` AC5), the manual ratification checkpoints (`TASK-260717-1dsqnj`, `TASK-260717-l639qp`, `TASK-260717-2d308k`), and the release-candidate approvals (`TASK-260715-1r48pc`, `TASK-260715-yynqbr`, `TASK-260715-2aessv`) are therefore separate, later interactions, not C1 content. |
