    {
      "id": "OC-1",
      "severity": "closed-was-architecture-blocking-for-macOS-credentials",
      "statement": "The macOS packet-tunnel system extension runs as root in a non-user context. The Data Protection Keychain is available only to code running in a user context, and keychain access groups share items between programs running as the SAME user. The .spec/security-privacy.md design — private keys and passphrases in the Data Protection Keychain shared through one access group with the packet tunnel extension — therefore CANNOT work between the macOS host and the macOS provider. It works unchanged on iOS.",
      "corroboration": "Observed 2026-07-28 on this Mac. (a) Two shipping Developer ID packet-tunnel system extensions ship com.apple.security.temporary-exception.files.absolute-path.read-write entries for /private/var/root/Library/Group Containers/ (Tailscale, Surfshark) — direct evidence that a root provider's group container is not the user's. (b) Surfshark's provider carries a temporary-exception path for /Library/Keychains/, i.e. the FILE-BASED System keychain, not the Data Protection Keychain. (c) PureVPN's providers carry keychain-access-groups outright. Together these indicate the real-world macOS pattern is a file-based/System keychain or an IPC seed, not a shared Data Protection Keychain access group.",
      "affects": [
        "TASK-260715-1o9wjz",
        "TASK-260715-379cpk",
        "TASK-260715-29ws8l",
        "TASK-260715-2hhh7x",
        "TASK-260715-3f4lxy"
      ],
      "matrixEffect": "closed. macos.provider keychain-access-groups is prohibited and settled; no other row is affected.",
      "status": "closed",
      "resolvedBy": "TASK-260728-7ii1xz",
      "resolvedIn": "2026-07-28.r4",
      "resolution": "Confirmed as stated and then resolved: the .spec/security-privacy.md shared Data-Protection-Keychain-access-group design does not work between the macOS host and a root provider, and works unchanged on iOS. The replacement is candidate D - the provider owns a file-based system-domain keychain item, seeded once from the host over NETunnelProviderSession.sendProviderMessage. Matrix consequence: macos.provider keychain-access-groups is settled prohibited (rule K1), not pending. Downstream tasks 379cpk, 1o9wjz, 29ws8l, 2hhh7x and 3f4lxy each revise the assumption named in TASK-260728-7ii1xz_macos-credential-transport-decision.md section 8."
    },
    {
      "id": "OC-2",
      "severity": "verify-at-first-build",
      "statement": "A sandboxed containing app carrying com.apple.developer.system-extension.install is reported working; the common failure mode is an invalid (not expired) provisioning profile producing a spurious 'would like to access data from other apps' prompt. Treat an invalid profile as the first hypothesis, not a sandbox incompatibility.",
      "corroboration": "All three shipping Developer ID hosts inspected on this Mac are NOT sandboxed, so none exercises this combination. The sandboxed-host plus system-extension.install shape is this project's own, from .spec/platform-distribution.md, and has no local precedent to lean on.",
