.temp/resources/TASK-260715-ypo7yo/TASK-260715-ypo7yo_apple-identifier-entitlement-matrix.json:5:  "revision": "2026-07-28.r6",
.temp/resources/TASK-260715-ypo7yo/TASK-260715-ypo7yo_apple-identifier-entitlement-matrix.json:6:  "supersedes": "2026-07-28.r5",
.temp/resources/TASK-260715-ypo7yo/TASK-260715-ypo7yo_apple-identifier-entitlement-matrix.json:9:  "humanAuthorizationNode": "TASK-260728-q5kjta",
.temp/resources/TASK-260715-ypo7yo/TASK-260715-ypo7yo_apple-identifier-entitlement-matrix.json:10:  "revisionLog": [
.temp/resources/TASK-260715-ypo7yo/TASK-260715-ypo7yo_apple-identifier-entitlement-matrix.json:12:      "revision": "2026-07-28.r6",
.temp/resources/TASK-260715-ypo7yo/TASK-260715-ypo7yo_apple-identifier-entitlement-matrix.json:18:      "summary": "Two contract-consistency corrections. No entitlement decision changed and no target row moved. F1: crossPlatformRules[2] still asserted the superseded r4 Keychain contract - the shared access group granted to the macOS host - while the target rows, rule K2, the record's consumedByTargets, assertions A6/A17 and the r5 revision log all said iOS-only. The sentence is corrected, and the DEFECT CLASS is closed rather than the string patched: new object crossPlatformSharingContract (rule S1) gives every cross-platform sentence a structured rule, and new gate R32 derives each grant set from the target rows, cross-checks it against the record's own consumer list, then requires the prose grantClause verbatim, requires the display names inside it to equal the derived set, and requires every other target named anywhere in the sentence to be a DECLARED non-grantee mention carrying its own clause. R33 requires the same grant clauses to appear in the human-readable rationale document, so the two artifacts cannot drift either. The second half of the class - a stale record COUNT - is closed by a scan for App Group count claims, each of which must sit at a registered path and agree with the record list; appGroupStyleRule's 'one record per family' was the surviving instance and is rewritten. F2: TASK-260715-3jloqy, the unattended task that spends the C1 authorization, still carried the pre-r5 capability contract - create shared groups, provision App Group and Keychain capabilities, AC1 requiring shared groups to exist, and AC2 requiring the packet-tunnel entitlement only on provider identifiers. Its description, scope, acceptance criteria and checklist are corrected on the board to c1AuthorizationScope exactly, and the requirement is pinned here as portalMutationTaskContract (rule P1) with a durable gate script, so the check verdict 05 ran by hand is reproducible.",
.temp/resources/TASK-260715-ypo7yo/TASK-260715-ypo7yo_apple-identifier-entitlement-matrix.json:19:      "verifiedIndependently": "Both findings were re-checked against the r5 artifact before being accepted, not taken on trust. F1: the reviewer's jq gate was re-run against r5 and returned false, exit 1; a sweep of EVERY string in the r5 JSON for stale-r4 grant or count claims then found exactly two survivors - crossPlatformRules[2] and appGroupStyleRule.statement's 'per family' - which is why the fix is a rule over all six sentences plus a count scan rather than two string edits. CONFIRMED. F2: TASK-260715-3jloqy's board record was read in full and all four quoted statements are present verbatim; its dependency edges confirm it is blocked by this matrix and by TASK-260728-q5kjta, so it executes after Ceremony C1 with exactly this contract in hand. The AC2 half was checked independently of the r5 changes: this contract's own target rows require com.apple.developer.networking.networkextension on all eight targets, hosts included, and R7 has enforced that since r2 - so 'only on provider identifiers' contradicted r1 as much as it contradicts r6, and would have under-provisioned both macOS host App IDs. CONFIRMED."
.temp/resources/TASK-260715-ypo7yo/TASK-260715-ypo7yo_apple-identifier-entitlement-matrix.json:22:      "revision": "2026-07-28.r5",
.temp/resources/TASK-260715-ypo7yo/TASK-260715-ypo7yo_apple-identifier-entitlement-matrix.json:29:      "summary": "Two least-privilege corrections and one board-metadata correction. F1: keychain-access-groups is now PROHIBITED on macos.host and therefore on all four macOS targets. New rule K2 states the test K1 did not cover - the entitlement's only function is sharing, and the macOS host has no second member to share with, because rule K1 excludes the root/system-domain provider and neither probe touches the Data Protection Keychain. The host keeps full access to its own items through the default access group the injected com.apple.application-identifier provides. K2 also records the ordering law that makes withholding safe and its one real consequence: the macOS default group moves from the shared literal to the app-ID literal, which TASK-260715-379cpk must be authored against. F3: com.apple.security.application-groups is now PROHIBITED on ios.probe.host and ios.probe.provider, because TASK-260715-1jckn0 scopes the probe to the NETunnelProviderManager lifecycle and versioned app messaging and names no shared container; G4 gains an explicit probeRule saying a probe inherits identifiers and profile classes but never entitlements. That left group.works.relux.tunnel.probe with no consumer, so the record is DELETED and G3 is restated as the stronger property that no probe carries a group at all. One App Group record remains. F2: TASK-260728-q5kjta's scope and AC4 were corrected on the board to name the same four macOS App IDs, Network Extensions only, zero App Group records and capabilities, no Keychain portal mutation, the Mac Development profile class, and all four excluded iOS App IDs. Every r2/r3/r4 decision the reviewer listed as acceptable is preserved.",
.temp/resources/TASK-260715-ypo7yo/TASK-260715-ypo7yo_apple-identifier-entitlement-matrix.json:33:      "revision": "2026-07-28.r4",
.temp/resources/TASK-260715-ypo7yo/TASK-260715-ypo7yo_apple-identifier-entitlement-matrix.json:40:      "summary": "com.apple.security.application-groups is now PROHIBITED on all four macOS targets and required on iOS only (new rule G4). The macOS credential transport question is closed: TASK-260728-7ii1xz selected the file-based system-domain keychain seeded over the NE app-message channel, with the non-secret snapshot travelling in providerConfiguration, and rejected the XPC candidate. None of the six grants the App Group entitlement actually confers on macOS is used by that design, so the capability has no current function on any macOS target and is withheld. Ceremony C1 no longer creates any App Group record or enables the App Groups capability on any macOS App ID. keychain-access-groups on macos.provider moves from prohibited-pending-decision to a settled prohibited, and loses the resolutionOwner and amendmentRule that existed only to keep it open; rule K1 replaces that amendmentRule with the correct, narrow reopening condition. OC-1 is closed; OC-4 is downgraded. Every accepted r2/r3 decision - the four production identifiers and their containment, channel-keyed Network Extension values, the registered iOS-style App Group literals, the legacy no-migration rule, the Sparkle Mach lookup exception and rule X1, profile classes, and C1's iOS exclusions - is preserved.",
.temp/resources/TASK-260715-ypo7yo/TASK-260715-ypo7yo_apple-identifier-entitlement-matrix.json:44:      "revision": "2026-07-28.r3",
.temp/resources/TASK-260715-ypo7yo/TASK-260715-ypo7yo_apple-identifier-entitlement-matrix.json:49:      "summary": "The macos.host entitlement set now carries com.apple.security.temporary-exception.mach-lookup.global-name with Sparkle's $(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)-spks / -spki values, attributed to project-architecture and owned by TASK-260715-uyju7n. Rule X1 adds a reviewed-exception register and an explicit prohibited set for Hardened Runtime relaxations, which is the same spec clause the finding exposed. Assertions A10e, A14 and A15 make all of it machine-checkable, and the r2 assertion set is pinned so it cannot silently shrink again. Every r2 decision \u2014 four production identifiers and containment, channel-keyed Network Extension values, identical registered iOS-style App Group literals, Keychain withholding on macos.provider, the legacy no-migration rule, C1 scope, the gap task, and dependencies \u2014 is preserved byte-for-byte.",
.temp/resources/TASK-260715-ypo7yo/TASK-260715-ypo7yo_apple-identifier-entitlement-matrix.json:53:      "revision": "2026-07-28.r2",
.temp/resources/TASK-260715-ypo7yo/TASK-260715-ypo7yo_apple-identifier-entitlement-matrix.json:188:      "allocationTiming": "Allocated on the Developer website when iOS re-arms under ADR-024, together with the four iOS App IDs that are this record's only consumers. Not created at Ceremony C1: no C1-authorized App ID needs the App Groups capability (rule G4), and creating a record no authorized identifier consumes is portal mutation without a function.",
.temp/resources/TASK-260715-ypo7yo/TASK-260715-ypo7yo_apple-identifier-entitlement-matrix.json:196:    "rationale": "Apple distinguishes two App Group ID styles. An iOS-style ID begins 'group.', is allocated on the Developer website, and is authorised by a provisioning profile. A macOS-style ID begins with the Team ID and CANNOT be explicitly allocated on the Developer website. Since 21 Feb 2025 iOS-style App Group IDs are fully supported on macOS for all product types, and Xcode 16.3 and later make the iOS style the macOS default. This project is new and builds with Xcode 26.5, so it takes the current, registrable style on both platforms. A team-prefixed macOS literal would not identify the portal record this contract says it consumes, which is the defect this revision closes.",
.temp/resources/TASK-260715-ypo7yo/TASK-260715-ypo7yo_apple-identifier-entitlement-matrix.json:447:          "rationale": "Settled by TASK-260728-7ii1xz. The selected transport is the file-based system-domain keychain, whose access-control model is per-item SecAccess ACLs, not access groups (Apple TN3137: 'The file-based keychain uses access control lists (SecAccess). The data protection keychain uses keychain access groups.'). The entitlement is therefore INAPPLICABLE to the mechanism actually used, not merely unnecessary. The provider needs no keychain entitlement at all: application.sb:792-793 already grants /Library/Keychains read+write to any process holding com.apple.developer.networking.networkextension, and application.sb:655-656 grant the securityd/SecurityServer Mach lookups to every sandboxed process. No new entitlement, no new App ID, no new portal capability, no change to Ceremony C1.",
.temp/resources/TASK-260715-ypo7yo/TASK-260715-ypo7yo_apple-identifier-entitlement-matrix.json:460:          "portalCapabilityNote": "App Sandbox is a build-time entitlement, not a portal App ID capability; it is excluded from the Ceremony C1 scope."
.temp/resources/TASK-260715-ypo7yo/TASK-260715-ypo7yo_apple-identifier-entitlement-matrix.json:499:      "deferral": "ADR-024 \u2014 iOS is deferred; identifiers are DEFINED so the branch resumes without renaming. Not authorized for portal creation at Ceremony C1.",
.temp/resources/TASK-260715-ypo7yo/TASK-260715-ypo7yo_apple-identifier-entitlement-matrix.json:1061:    "history": "Reviewer verdict 05 F1. Through r5 the cross-platform summary was six free-text strings that nothing checked. r5 corrected eight target rows, two rules, two assertions and the C1 scope, and left crossPlatformRules[2] asserting the superseded r4 contract - that the shared Keychain access group is granted to the macOS host. The 846-check r5 validator passed, because no rule looked at the summary at all. The defect class is a prose summary that ASSERTS a grant set instead of RENDERING one; the fix is to make every such sentence a checked projection of the data rather than a second, unsynchronised statement of it.",
.temp/resources/TASK-260715-ypo7yo/TASK-260715-ypo7yo_apple-identifier-entitlement-matrix.json:1067:      "revisionLog",
.temp/resources/TASK-260715-ypo7yo/TASK-260715-ypo7yo_apple-identifier-entitlement-matrix.json:1072:    "historicalPathsNote": "These paths describe the state of an EARLIER revision on purpose and are pinned to it, so a count they mention is not a claim about the current data. Everything else is scanned. The exclusion list is declared here rather than hard-coded in the validator, so widening it is a visible edit to the contract.",
.temp/resources/TASK-260715-ypo7yo/TASK-260715-ypo7yo_apple-identifier-entitlement-matrix.json:1199:    "statement": "The unattended portal mutation task consumes the human authorization granted at Ceremony C1, so its board contract must state the SAME mutation set as c1AuthorizationScope. Correcting the authorization node alone does not correct the task that spends the authorization.",
.temp/resources/TASK-260715-ypo7yo/TASK-260715-ypo7yo_apple-identifier-entitlement-matrix.json:1200:    "history": "Reviewer verdict 05 F2. r5 corrected TASK-260728-q5kjta - the human ceremony - to the post-G4/K2 scope, and left TASK-260715-3jloqy, the task that actually performs the mutations, carrying the pre-r5 contract: create 'shared groups', provision 'packet-tunnel, App Group, and Keychain capabilities', AC1 requiring that 'shared groups exist', and AC2 requiring the packet-tunnel entitlement 'only on provider identifiers'. The last of those is a material defect independent of App Groups: Apple requires com.apple.developer.networking.networkextension on the containing app as well as the embedded provider, so a provider-only rule would under-provision both macOS host App IDs and fail the build this matrix exists to make possible.",
.temp/resources/TASK-260715-ypo7yo/TASK-260715-ypo7yo_apple-identifier-entitlement-matrix.json:1396:      "notAStopTheLine": "The question is only reachable if a rejected candidate is revived, it has a named owner, and it does not block Ceremony C1 - C1 no longer grants the App Groups capability at all."
.temp/resources/TASK-260715-ypo7yo/TASK-260715-ypo7yo_apple-identifier-entitlement-matrix.json:1419:    "capabilityValueNote": "The portal capability is enabled per App ID by NAME ('Network Extensions'); the entitlement VALUES that end up authorized are a property of the profile class then generated. Because C1 creates Mac Development profiles only, the values C1 puts in circulation are the unsuffixed ones. The '-systemextension' values enter only with the Developer ID profiles owned by TASK-260715-3gkwn0, outside C1. The operator is therefore never asked to select an entitlement value at C1.",
.temp/resources/TASK-260715-ypo7yo/TASK-260715-ypo7yo_apple-identifier-entitlement-matrix.json:1430:    "appGroupNote": "r5: Ceremony C1 creates NO App Group record and enables the App Groups capability on NO App ID. Rule G4 prohibits com.apple.security.application-groups on every macOS target, so no C1-authorized App ID needs it. Only ONE App Group record now exists in this contract, group.works.relux.tunnel, whose consumers are the two production iOS App IDs that C1 explicitly does not authorize; it is allocated when iOS re-arms under ADR-024. The probe record was deleted in r5 when the iOS probe pair lost the entitlement (verdict 04 F3).",
.temp/resources/TASK-260715-ypo7yo/TASK-260715-ypo7yo_apple-identifier-entitlement-matrix.json:1431:    "keychainNote": "r5: C1 authorizes NO Keychain access group mutation, and never did require one - keychain sharing has no portal record (keychainAccessGroups[].portalNote). Rule K2 additionally removes the group from macos.host, so no C1-authorized App ID declares keychain-access-groups at all. An operator asked to 'create the Keychain access group' at C1 is being asked for a mutation that does not exist."
.temp/resources/TASK-260715-ypo7yo/TASK-260715-ypo7yo_apple-identifier-entitlement-matrix.json:1433:  "amendmentRule": "This matrix is amended only by TASK-260715-ypo7yo (or a successor task that explicitly assumes ownership), on the strength of a named resolving task. An amendment MUST update this JSON, bump revision, append to revisionLog, keep validate_matrix.py passing, and state which open constraint it closes. Downstream tasks do not locally reinterpret a row; they raise a blocker against the owner.",
.temp/resources/TASK-260715-ypo7yo/TASK-260715-ypo7yo_apple-identifier-entitlement-matrix.json:1435:    "TASK-260728-q5kjta \u2014 Ceremony C1 authorization scope",
.temp/resources/TASK-260715-ypo7yo/TASK-260715-ypo7yo_apple-identifier-entitlement-matrix.json:1506:    "ceremonyCostIsZero": "Withholding introduces no new human ceremony, which removes the operational argument verdict 03 rejected. The four iOS App IDs are already in c1AuthorizationScope.explicitlyNotAuthorized under ADR-024, so iOS provisioning already requires a later portal sitting that C1 does not cover. The App Group record is allocated at that sitting, alongside the iOS App IDs that are its only consumers. Nothing that C1 could have granted today is needed today. (r6: this sentence counted r4's two records until rule S1's count scan caught it - the third instance of the stale-summary class reviewer verdict 05 F1 named, and the one neither the reviewer nor r5 had spotted.)",
.temp/resources/TASK-260715-ypo7yo/TASK-260715-ypo7yo_apple-identifier-entitlement-matrix.json:1521:      "owner": "TASK-260728-q5kjta",
.temp/resources/TASK-260715-ypo7yo/TASK-260715-ypo7yo_apple-identifier-entitlement-matrix.json:1522:      "consequence": "Ceremony C1 shrinks, and its board text was corrected in r5 to match this scope (verdict 04 F2). The sitting authorizes exactly four macOS App IDs - works.relux.tunnel.mac, works.relux.tunnel.mac.tunnel, works.relux.tunnel.probe.mac, works.relux.tunnel.probe.mac.tunnel - the Network Extensions capability on those four, and one Mac Development profile each with this Mac registered. It creates NO App Group record, enables the App Groups capability on NO App ID, and performs NO Keychain access group mutation (there is no such portal record). All four iOS App IDs are explicitly excluded under ADR-024. Fewer portal mutations, same single sitting."
.temp/resources/TASK-260715-ypo7yo/TASK-260715-ypo7yo_apple-identifier-entitlement-matrix.json:1526:      "consequence": "r6, verdict 05 F2: this task's own board contract was corrected, because it is the task that spends the C1 authorization unattended. Its description, scope, AC and checklist now state exactly portalMutationTaskContract (rule P1) - four macOS App IDs, Network Extensions on all four INCLUDING both hosts, one Mac Development profile each, no App Group record, no App Groups capability, no Keychain portal mutation, no iOS App ID, no distribution profile. The removed AC2 clause 'the packet-tunnel entitlement appears only on provider identifiers' was a material defect: Apple requires the entitlement on the containing app as well as the embedded provider, and R7 has required it on all eight target rows since r2, so provider-only verification would have passed a portal state that cannot build. Portal verification must also assert two NEGATIVES. App Groups: none of the four macOS App IDs has the capability enabled and no Mac Development profile's Entitlements dict carries com.apple.security.application-groups (assertion A16). Keychain: no macOS bundle declares keychain-access-groups (assertion A17). A17 is asserted against the signed BUNDLE, not the profile - a Mac Development profile legitimately carries the team-wide keychain-access-groups = ['<TEAMID>.*'] wildcard, and that wildcard is not a finding."
.temp/resources/TASK-260715-ypo7yo/TASK-260715-ypo7yo_apple-identifier-entitlement-matrix.json:1538:      "consequence": "The macOS profile snapshot loader cannot read an App Group container and cannot fall back to one. On macOS the non-secret snapshot arrives in providerConfiguration; on iOS the App Group container remains correct. This is the same revision TASK-260728-7ii1xz section 8 already assigns."
.temp/resources/TASK-260715-ypo7yo/TASK-260715-ypo7yo_apple-identifier-entitlement-matrix.json:1542:      "consequence": "PlatformVPNIdentity (Sources/ReluxTunnelCore/VPNManagerRepository.swift:17) declares appGroupIdentifier as a non-optional String for both platforms, and production(for:) still throws. When that binding is authored there is no macOS App Group value to supply. The shape must change - most plainly to an optional or a platform-keyed identity - and this matrix is the input that forces it. No product source was changed by this revision."

