71:### 0730 — Entitlement values are a property of the signing channel, not of the target (TASK-260715-ypo7yo, r2)
72-- ROOT CAUSE: The r1 matrix assigned `packet-tunnel-provider-systemextension` to all four macOS targets unconditionally while authorizing **Mac Development** profiles for the same four App IDs. Apple DTS is explicit that the value follows the distribution channel: the `-systemextension` suffix is for direct Developer ID signing, and "otherwise — including when you distribute the app on the App Store and when signing for development — use the values without that suffix" (forums/thread/800887). Ceremony C1 would have provisioned development profiles against a value development signing never uses, and C1 is a one-shot human node (ADR-028).
73-- FINDING: Verified against three shipping Developer-ID packet-tunnel products installed on this Mac — Tailscale, PureVPN, Surfshark. All three carry the suffixed value on **both** the containing app and the embedded sysex, confirming the Developer ID half. No development-signed counterpart exists locally, so the development half rests on the DTS statement and is re-checked by assertion A3 at first signed build.
74-- DECISION: Every NE value in the contract is now keyed by channel (`development` / `developer-id` / `app-store`), each profile row records `authorizesNetworkExtensionValues`, and the C1 scope is pinned to development-channel values so the ceremony cannot be pointed at the wrong one. Consequence worth remembering: the probe family is development-signed only, so it can prove the **development** entitlement shape and nothing else — r1 implied it proved the production shape.
75-
76:### 0726 — Apple does not require the containing app of an NE provider to be sandboxed (TASK-260715-ypo7yo, r2)
77-- FINDING: Read `com.apple.security.app-sandbox` off three shipping Developer-ID host/provider pairs on this Mac: hosts `false` in all three (Tailscale, PureVPN, Surfshark), providers `true` in all three. Apple requires the **provider system extension** to be sandboxed; the containing app is not covered by that rule for direct distribution. r1 attributed both to a single Apple requirement.
78-- DECISION: `ReluxProxyMac`'s sandbox is recorded as a **project** decision — `.spec/platform-distribution.md` embeds Sparkle only in the sandboxed hardened-runtime host with `SUEnableInstallerLauncherService` and the `-spks`/`-spki` Mach exceptions, per ADR-018 — not an Apple rule. The contract now carries `requirementSource` per row (`apple-requirement` / `project-architecture` / `sandbox-consequence` / `platform-inherent`) and validator R21 rejects a host row claiming an Apple requirement.
79-- NOTE: This sharpens OC-2. None of the three shipping products is a sandboxed host, so the sandboxed-host + `system-extension.install` combination has **no local precedent** to lean on; `9yp8to` is genuinely first through that door.
80-
81:### 0722 — iOS-style App Group IDs are the correct macOS choice since Feb 2025, with one unverified corner (TASK-260715-ypo7yo, r2)
82:- ROOT CAUSE: r1 registered `group.works.relux.tunnel` but had macOS claim `262RZ595FP.group.works.relux.tunnel`, calling it "the same record with a team prefix". Those are two different Apple identifier *styles*, not two spellings of one record: iOS-style begins `group.` and is allocated on the Developer website; macOS-style begins with the Team ID and **cannot** be allocated there at all. The macOS literal therefore did not identify the portal record the contract said it consumed.
83-- FINDING: Apple completed Developer-website support for iOS-style app group IDs on macOS on **21 Feb 2025**, and Xcode 16.3+ makes it the macOS default (forums/thread/721701). This project is new on Xcode 26.5, so both platforms now claim the identical registered literal.
84-- ANOMALY: Every Apple example of an app group used as a sandboxed **Mach service name prefix**, and all three shipping products inspected here, use the macOS-style Team-ID form — and all predate Feb 2025. No source found confirms an iOS-style ID works as a Mach service prefix. Not blocking: the spec's host↔provider channel is an App Group snapshot plus Keychain references (`.spec/architecture.md:111`), not XPC. Recorded as **OC-4** owned by `TASK-260728-7ii1xz`, with the amendment path being a separate *unregistered* macOS-style ID for that one purpose. This supersedes the 0657 claim below that the macOS group is required "as the XPC Mach-service namespace" — that purpose is now conditional, not established.
85-- FINDING: The r1 D-2 rationale ("the entitlement would be non-functional") was too strong. PureVPN's root sysexes carry `keychain-access-groups` outright and Surfshark's carries a `/Library/Keychains/` path exception — i.e. the **file-based System keychain** works from root. What fails is specifically the *Data Protection* Keychain sharing the spec asks for. The row stays withheld because its purpose is undetermined until `7ii1xz` picks a transport, not because it is inert.
86-
87:### 0718 — A containment assertion that ignores signing-generated entitlements is unrunnable (TASK-260715-ypo7yo, r2)
88-- ROOT CAUSE: r1's assertion A10 rejected "any entitlement key outside the matrix union", but the matrix authors only project capabilities. The signing toolchain injects more, so A10 would have failed a legitimately signed development bundle — or forced undocumented local exceptions, which is worse.
89-- FINDING: Established empirically with `codesign -d --entitlements :-` over four signed macOS bundles and `security cms -D` over a local Mac Development profile. macOS always gets `com.apple.application-identifier` and `com.apple.developer.team-identifier`; debug builds additionally get `com.apple.security.get-task-allow`, stripped on distribution export. Note the macOS spellings carry the `com.apple.` prefix where iOS does not. A Mac Development profile authorizes `keychain-access-groups` as a team-wide `<TEAMID>.*` wildcard, which is why the shared keychain group needs no portal record of its own.
90-- DECISION: A10 became four assertions over a computed allowlist — completeness, containment, exclusion, channel hygiene — where the allowlist is authored keys ∪ always-injected ∪ (development-only if that channel). iOS spellings are recorded at **medium confidence** with `3dno4w` named as verifier, since no iOS bundle was built here; guessing them silently would have been the same class of error.
91-- VERIFICATION: 24 rules / 537 checks over the JSON, exit 0, plus **23 negative gates** that each re-introduce a defect and must fail closed naming their rule (6 for the NE-channel defect, 4 App Group, 3 allowlist, 3 sandbox attribution, 7 re-checking preserved r1 decisions). The r1 harness mutated the JSON with regex substitution, which can silently no-op; r2 mutates the parsed object, so a mutation that fails to apply is itself reported as a gate failure.
92-
93:### 0657 — The macOS provider runs as root, so the specified Keychain sharing cannot work (TASK-260715-ypo7yo)
94-- BLOCKER: `.spec/security-privacy.md` requires private keys and passphrases to live in the Data Protection Keychain "shared only through the minimum keychain access group required by the app and its packet tunnel extension" (also `.spec/threat-model.md` DF-02/M-02). On macOS that is unsatisfiable. Developer ID direct distribution forces the provider to be a **system extension** (TN3134, `.spec/platform-distribution.md`), a sysex runs **as root** outside a user context, the Data Protection Keychain is available only to code in a user context, and keychain access groups share items between programs running as the **same** user. The host runs as the logged-in user. iOS is unaffected — its appex runs as the same user as its host.
95-- FINDING: The same root/user split kills the App Group **container** as a macOS host↔provider channel: the sysex's container resolves under `/var/root/Library/Group Containers`, the host's under the user's home. App Groups are still **required** on macOS, but only as the XPC Mach-service namespace a sandboxed process must prefix with — not as shared storage. `TASK-260715-3f4lxy` (profile snapshot loader) inherits this.
96-- DECISION: The matrix withholds `keychain-access-groups` from `works.relux.tunnel.mac.tunnel` (`prohibited-pending-decision`) rather than provisioning a right the provider cannot exercise. Created `TASK-260728-7ii1xz` to select the macOS credential transport and return a grant-or-keep-prohibited amendment; it blocks `379cpk`, `1o9wjz`, `29ws8l`, `3f4lxy`. Five credential tasks all assumed the shared-group model and none owned the question of whether it holds.
97-- NOTE: The trap that made this invisible is that the entitlement is *accepted* everywhere — portal, profile, and `codesign` all succeed. Only a runtime `SecItem` call in the provider fails, which is M1-deep. Least privilege caught it because "grant only what the target can actually use" forced the question.
98-
99:### 0653 — Target name is not bundle identifier, and that is a legacy-collision hazard (TASK-260715-ypo7yo)
100:- DECISION: Apple identifiers are `works.relux.tunnel.{mac,mac.tunnel,ios,ios.tunnel}` plus a disjoint disposable probe family `works.relux.tunnel.probe.*`, under Team `262RZ595FP` (Relux Works, LLC). Provider identifier is always host identifier + `.tunnel`, so containment is derivable in both directions with no lookup table.
101-- FINDING: The generated target *names* from `.spec/platform-distribution.md` are `ReluxProxyMac`/`ReluxProxyIOS`. Any generator that derives a bundle identifier from the target name emits `works.relux.proxy.mac` — inside the shipped legacy `works.relux.proxy` identity namespace, which is exactly the silent-repurposing failure AC4 forbids. Target name and bundle identifier are explicitly decoupled and assertion A11 (`^works\.relux\.proxy(\.|$)`) exists to catch it.
102-- DECISION: One bundle identifier per target across every build configuration — no `.debug`/`.dev` variants. macOS system-extension approval is keyed to Team ID + extension bundle identifier, so suffixing multiplies physical approval friction on Holds H2/H3, and `9yp8to` must exercise the identity that ships. Environment is expressed by signing identity class, profile class, hardened runtime, and `get-task-allow`. The single exception is the probe family, which exists so throwaway sysex approvals never burn the production identity.
103-- FINDING: Probe identifiers had to be in this matrix, not invented later by `1r0fxv`. C1 authorizes "exactly the approved macOS entries"; had the probe been absent, provisioning it would have needed a second human sitting — the thing ADR-028 forbids.
--
424-### 0351 — Owned VPN preferences are shared policy with thin platform clients (TASK-260715-15vkvz)
425-- DECISION: `ReluxTunnelCore` owns the serialized exact-predicate repository, immutable canonical configuration, 15-second callback tokens, stable errors, one-stale retry, and reload verification. The iOS/macOS adapter modules only translate public `NetworkExtension` objects and notifications into the injectable core seams.
426-- SAFETY: Ensure never enables; the explicit enable result names the OS-level possibility that another enterprise VPN is disabled. Duplicate repair removes every freshly revalidated current-schema inactive exact-owned manager, reloads after each removal, proves zero, then recreates one. Unmarked, unrelated, wrong-type, corrupt duplicate, active duplicate, and future-owned managers are zero-write paths.
427:- IDENTITY: The complete host/provider/App Group/Keychain `PlatformVPNIdentity` remains injectable for deterministic fixtures. Both production factories fail closed until `TASK-260715-ypo7yo` supplies accepted signed bindings; no guessed identifier landed.
428-- VERIFICATION: 18 deterministic Swift Testing cases cover mixed managers, type confusion, least-data/secret exclusion, idempotency, explicit enable/disable/remove, duplicate repair, future preservation, stale replacement, authorization, save/reload failure, timeout/cancellation late callbacks, and stop timeout. `make validate-core` passes 236 tests in 24 suites plus build; strict format lint and iOS Simulator/macOS Xcode builds pass.
429-
430-### 0320 — Provider routing and cleanup share one bounded generation owner (TASK-260715-1bp6eu)
--
438-- DECISION: A manager is owned only when its protocol is `NETunnelProviderProtocol`, its provider bundle ID exactly matches the accepted platform binding, and its stable provider-configuration marker matches. Labels, server address, enabled state, profile UUID, schema version, and array order never establish ownership. Every write/remove rechecks a freshly loaded object; unmarked lookalikes and duplicates are zero-write conflicts outside explicit inactive duplicate repair.
439-- PLATFORM FINDING: Apple limits `loadAllFromPreferences` to configurations created by the calling app, but enabling an enterprise VPN can cause the OS to disable another app's enterprise VPN. Therefore ordinary ensure preserves/creates a disabled configuration and explicit foreground user intent owns enable/save/reload. Relux never mutates the unrelated manager object and reports the system-level enable effect truthfully.
440-- AUTHORITY: `NETunnelProviderSession.status` is the sole system-session authority; a fresh generation/sequence provider snapshot is the sole runtime capability authority. Host suspension/termination removes observers only. Provider start/stop once-gates and exact deadlines remain extension-owned and join the accepted coordinator cleanup.
441:- RESIDUAL: Exact four-target bundle IDs, App Group, Keychain group, and entitlements are not yet accepted (`TASK-260715-ypo7yo`). Shared implementation proceeds through injected identities, while production composition fails closed instead of inventing values. Human ratification stays decoupled in `TASK-260717-1dsqnj`.
442-
443-### 0211 — Coordinator fault suite freezes ownership boundaries and resource baselines (TASK-260715-32virr)
444-- DECISION: Fault injection uses the coordinator's injected cancellation-check seam to fail at fourteen exact before/after ownership boundaries. This keeps production code unchanged while proving reverse rollback, one disconnecting publication, one terminal publication, exact error mapping, and single cleanup for configuration, SSH, TCP, DNS, packet plane, settings, and packet-read ownership.

